  <div class="prose">
    <p>Watch decimal feet. 5.5 ft is 5 ft 6 in, not 5 ft 5 in, because the decimal is a fraction of 12 inches, not 10. Tape measures and plans that use decimal feet (common in surveying and engineering) need this conversion before you cut anything.</p>

    <h2>Running distances</h2>
    <p>Race distances are set in kilometers, so the mile figures are the converted ones: a 5K is 3.107 miles, a 10K is 6.214 miles, a half marathon (21.0975 km) is 13.109 miles, and a marathon (42.195 km) is 26.219 miles. The familiar "26.2" is that last figure rounded.</p>

    <h2>Converting in your head</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Inches to centimeters:</strong> multiply by 2.5 (exact factor 2.54). 12 in → 30 cm (exact: 30.48).</li>
      <li><strong>Kilometers to miles:</strong> multiply by 5/8. 80 km/h → 50 mph (exact: 49.7).</li>
      <li><strong>Miles to kilometers:</strong> multiply by 1.6. 30 miles → 48 km (exact: 48.3).</li>
      <li><strong>Meters to feet:</strong> a meter is a yard plus about 10%, or 3.28 ft. 100 m → 328 ft.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Which "foot" and which "mile"</h2>
    <p>Since 1959 the international inch has been exactly 2.54 cm, which makes the foot exactly 0.3048 m. The U.S. survey foot, slightly longer at 1200/3937 m, lingered in land surveying until it was officially retired at the end of 2022. The difference is about 2 parts per million, or roughly 1 foot every 100 miles: irrelevant for a room, relevant for old state-plane coordinates.</p>
    <p>A nautical mile (1,852 m) is 1.151 statute miles. It is used at sea and in aviation, and a knot is 1 nautical mile per hour, so a boat at 20 knots is making about 23 mph.</p>
  </div>
  <section class="hub-faq" aria-labelledby="guide-faq-title"><h2 id="guide-faq-title">Length conversion questions</h2>
    <details><summary>How many centimeters are in an inch?</summary><p>Exactly 2.54 centimeters. A foot is exactly 30.48 cm.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How tall is 170 cm in feet and inches?</summary><p>170 cm is 66.9 inches, or about 5 feet 7 inches (5′6.9″ before rounding).</p></details>
    <details><summary>How many feet are in a meter?</summary><p>1 meter is 3.2808 feet, or about 3 feet 3.4 inches.</p></details>
  </section>
